Z||000000||fiero56||11-30-1999||12:31 AM||||Looks like I'm in the market for a new EGR tube.  Who makes/carries the best one?  I want one that isn'tgoing to break over time(yeah right).  Should I just go with the Fiero Store? I've heard IRM and some other company also carry this tube. What should I do... Oh, what should I do?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||11-30-1999||07:37 AM||||I bought the IRM one a few years ago before FOCOA started selling one.  I've been very pleased with it.  It was a bit pricey at $90.00 though.  I believe FOCOA's is around $70.00 or so.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||11-30-1999||04:02 PM||||I just plugged mine off and it works fine. Take the small vacuum line off of the top of the valve and plug off the tube with a rubber thing. Then start your car and it will idle perfect and you will gain 53 HP, This was dynoed too. Just kidding about the last part.||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||12-01-1999||06:50 PM||||Formula,<P>Plugging it off might work well for a while.  This is not a long term solution by any means.  Unfortunately, our engines were designed with EGR.  Contrary to what some think, this actually cools down the air after the vehicle is warmed up.  Since you are not cooling down the air, you will most likely bake your engine into a non-working order.  ||||reg||4
Z||000004||fiero56||12-01-1999||09:04 PM||||I've heard people go both ways. I'll stick with mine, but some people say it works without.  Ed Parks (THE Fiero man) says Fiero's are very good at burning a hole in cyl#2(v-6), especially when they have no EGR. I bought a new tube from the Fiero Store, another $100 or so to add to my "extra expenses" list.||||reg||1
